# Every environment variable Hearth reads, each with a comment saying what it's for.
#
# `npm run dev` reads this file and then .env (git-ignored), whose values win, so the values here
# are the local Compose stack's (docker-compose.yml) and work as they are once `npm run db:reset`
# has built its database. None of them is a
# secret: they only reach containers bound to 127.0.0.1. Real secrets never go in this file or in
# .env files in the repo; in AWS the values come from the task definition and Secrets Manager
# (C0.11). CI's start-up check (scripts/check-server.sh) reads this file too, so a variable the
# server needs can't be missing from it.
#
# Server-only variables never start with VITE_: Vite builds VITE_* variables into the browser
# bundle.

# --- Server (apps/server) ---

# Which part of the server this process runs: api, realtime or worker (one image, T2).
# ROLE=api
# The address and port the server listens on. The image sets HOST=0.0.0.0 so the load balancer
# can reach it. Locally, run realtime beside the api on 3001 (`ROLE=realtime PORT=3001 npm run
# dev`): the web app's dev server (`npm run dev -w @hearth/web`) proxies /api to 3000 and /ws to
# 3001 (T21).
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# PORT=3000
# How much the server logs: fatal, error, warn, info, debug, trace or silent.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# The web app's origin, exactly: scheme, host and port, with no path or trailing slash. The server
# refuses any request that changes state unless its Origin header is this, WebKit's CSP reports
# aside (Origin null with Sec-Fetch-Site same-origin: TECH-SPEC T21 item 9; CLAUDE.md, Security
# and privacy). Locally, the Vite dev server's address (C0.8); the e2e suite sets its own,
# https://localhost:5180, for the servers it starts (T21).
APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# --- Postgres (docker-compose.yml: postgres) ---

# The server's database, as the runtime role hearth_app, which owns no table and is held to row
# level security (CLAUDE.md, Database). `npm run db:reset` builds the local `hearth` database and
# gives hearth_app this local-only password; in AWS it signs in with IAM instead (C0.12).
# Only the api and realtime roles read it; the worker never connects as hearth_app.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://hearth_app:hearth-local@127.0.0.1:5432/hearth

# --- The worker (apps/server, ROLE=worker; TECH-SPEC T15, T18) ---

# The worker's database login, hearth_worker: it holds nothing itself and takes one job role per
# transaction. Only the worker reads it; the api and realtime roles connect with DATABASE_URL, and
# the worker never does. `npm run db:reset` gives it this local-only password; in AWS it signs in
# with IAM (C0.12), and each task gets only its own login (C0.11).
WORKER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://hearth_worker:hearth-local@127.0.0.1:5432/hearth
# How many jobs one worker process runs at once (1 to 32). Its work pool has this many
# connections, plus one per outbox consumer; its control pool has 8 more.
# WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4

# --- Migrations (apps/server/src/db/migrate.ts, the pre-deploy step) ---

# Where the migrate step connects, and the role every migration runs as. Only the migrate step
# reads these, never the server. The login is hearth_owner itself, or a NOINHERIT login whose
# only membership is hearth_owner, so a migration that switches role gains nothing; without a URL
# the standard PG* variables say where to connect. Locally scripts/db-local.sh (npm run db:reset
# and db:migrate) connects as the Compose superuser itself, so they stay unset here.
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://hearth_owner@127.0.0.1:5432/hearth
# MIGRATION_ROLE=hearth_owner

# --- Valkey (docker-compose.yml: valkey) ---

# redis:// locally; rediss:// (TLS) for ElastiCache for Valkey in AWS.
VALKEY_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379

# --- Sign-in (apps/server/src/auth; TECH-SPEC T14, T20) ---

# Where this server runs: local (the Compose stack), test (automated tests), nonprod or prod.
# Settings meant only for this machine are refused anywhere else.
DEPLOY_ENV=local
# The api role's sign-in secret, at least 32 characters. The keys for sign-in codes and for the
# hashed phone numbers in Valkey, and the PIN pepper, are derived from it. This value is the
# local stack's: it is refused unless DEPLOY_ENV is local or test and APP_ORIGIN is a localhost
# origin. In AWS it comes from Secrets Manager (C0.12), one per environment. Changing it makes
# every PIN need an admin reset until C0.12 adds rotation.
AUTH_SECRET=hearth-local-hearth-local-hearth-local-hearth
# How sign-in codes are sent. fake: this machine only (DEPLOY_ENV local or test and a localhost
# APP_ORIGIN); a stand-in for Twilio Verify that prints the code in the terminal, never in the
# JSON logs. twilio: Twilio Verify, nonprod and prod only, never with a localhost APP_ORIGIN; it
# sends nothing until C0.9 clears Twilio in the transfer register (PDPA-10), and C0.12 stores its
# secrets. Unset: no code can be sent, so sign-in answers "unavailable" (TECH-SPEC T7, T20).
SMS_PROVIDER=fake
# Twilio Verify, only with SMS_PROVIDER=twilio, from Secrets Manager in AWS (C0.12); never set
# here. The Restricted API Key's SID (SK and 32 hex digits), scoped to Verify:
# TWILIO_API_KEY_SID=
# That API key's secret (32 letters and digits):
# TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET=
# The Verify Service's SID (VA and 32 hex digits):
# TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=
# The proxies in front of the server whose X-Forwarded-For to trust, as a comma-separated list of
# IP addresses or CIDR ranges (a hop count isn't accepted). Empty locally; in AWS the load
# balancer's subnets and CloudFront's origin-facing ranges (C0.12).
# TRUST_PROXY=

# --- Object storage (docker-compose.yml: s3) ---

# The bucket the server stores files in, which /ready checks. The Compose stack creates it.
S3_BUCKET=hearth-local
# The AWS region of the bucket (ap-southeast-1, D37).
S3_REGION=ap-southeast-1
# Local only: the Compose stack's S3-compatible store, which needs path-style URLs. Leave both
# unset in AWS, where the SDK uses S3's own endpoint.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9000
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# Local only: the store's dummy keys. Leave both unset in AWS, where the ECS task role signs
# requests and no key is ever stored.
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=hearth-local
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=hearth-local-secret
